% Test cases for the surgical dressings policy (surgical_dressings.le):
% synthetic claims, one or two per criterion of LCD L33831 and per payment
% condition of dmepos.le and Policy Article A54563, each stating only what
% its record shows. Findings are recorded as the treating practitioner's,
% the reviewer's or the Council's; a finding nobody made leaves the answer
% conditional. Dressings are a one-off supply or a refill. The queries:
% whether the claim is payable (the first query, so "stage" reads its
% checklist), whether it is reasonable and necessary, and what minimal
% change would make it payable.

the target language is: prolog.

the knowledge base surgical_dressings cases includes these resources:
    surgical_dressings.

the knowledge base surgical_dressings cases includes:

query pay is:
    which claim is payable.

query rn is:
    which claim is reasonable and necessary.

query stage is:
    the query fails at which section.

query flip is:
    which minimal change to the scenario makes it the case that
        claim 1 is payable.

% A complete claim for a month of bordered foam covers (A6212, three times a
% week: 12) as the primary dressing of a debrided stage 3 sacral pressure
% ulcer with moderate exudate: the order of 20 February, the evaluation of
% 25 February, delivery and proof of delivery. The Surgical Dressings
% benefit asks nothing about the residence; no code of the policy is on the
% CMS face-to-face list.
scenario foam_complete is:
    claim 1 is for the foam covers.
    the HCPCS code of the foam covers is "A6212".
    the foam covers is furnished to Ann.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 12.
    the use of the foam covers is primary dressing.
    the foam covers is applied to the sacral ulcer.
    the sacral ulcer is a wound of Ann.
    the type of the sacral ulcer is pressure ulcer.
    the stage of the sacral ulcer is 3.
    the exudate of the sacral ulcer is moderate.
    the sacral ulcer has been debrided.
    the sacral ulcer was evaluated on 2026-02-25.
    the order for the foam covers is dated 2026-02-20.
    the order for the foam covers is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the foam covers includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the foam covers includes order date.
    the order for the foam covers includes description of the item.
    the order for the foam covers includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the foam covers includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the foam covers was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-22.
    the foam covers was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the foam covers.
    pay expects answers ["claim 1 is payable"].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers [].

% The criteria of the categories: an alginate cover on Ben's heavily
% exudative stage 4 ulcer (claim 1, covered); on Cy's dry stage 3 ulcer
% (claim 2, not covered); a hydrocolloid on Dee's heavily draining wound
% (claim 3, not covered); a hydrogel cover on Eve's stage 2 ulcer (claim 4,
% not covered); a transparent film on Fay's closed incision (claim 5,
% covered); saline impregnated gauze on Ben's ulcer (claim 6, never
% covered).
scenario category_criteria is:
    claim 1 is for the alginate covers.
    the HCPCS code of the alginate covers is "A6196".
    the alginate covers is applied to the heel ulcer.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 30.
    the heel ulcer is a wound of Ben.
    the type of the heel ulcer is pressure ulcer.
    the stage of the heel ulcer is 4.
    the exudate of the heel ulcer is heavy.
    claim 2 is for the second alginate covers.
    the HCPCS code of the second alginate covers is "A6196".
    the second alginate covers is applied to the dry ulcer.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 30.
    the dry ulcer is a wound of Cy.
    the type of the dry ulcer is pressure ulcer.
    the stage of the dry ulcer is 3.
    the exudate of the dry ulcer is none.
    claim 3 is for the hydrocolloid covers.
    the HCPCS code of the hydrocolloid covers is "A6234".
    the hydrocolloid covers is applied to the leg wound.
    claim 3 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 3 is 12.
    the leg wound is a wound of Dee.
    the type of the leg wound is surgical wound.
    the exudate of the leg wound is heavy.
    claim 4 is for the hydrogel covers.
    the HCPCS code of the hydrogel covers is "A6242".
    the hydrogel covers is applied to the hip ulcer.
    claim 4 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 4 is 30.
    the hip ulcer is a wound of Eve.
    the type of the hip ulcer is pressure ulcer.
    the stage of the hip ulcer is 2.
    the thickness of the hip ulcer is partial thickness.
    the exudate of the hip ulcer is minimal.
    claim 5 is for the film.
    the HCPCS code of the film is "A6257".
    the film is applied to the incision.
    claim 5 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 5 is 12.
    the incision is a wound of Fay.
    the type of the incision is surgical wound.
    the incision is closed.
    claim 6 is for the saline gauze.
    the HCPCS code of the saline gauze is "A6228".
    the saline gauze is applied to the heel ulcer.
    claim 6 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 6 is 30.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 5 is reasonable and necessary"].

% The usual quantities: gauze pads without a border on Gus's surgical
% wound, three a day — 90 for a month (claim 1, covered), 100 (claim 2, not
% covered); bordered specialty absorptive dressings on Hal's two stage 3
% ulcers, every other day each — 30 for a month (claim 3, covered), 40
% (claim 4, not covered).
scenario usual_quantities is:
    claim 1 is for the gauze pads.
    the HCPCS code of the gauze pads is "A6216".
    the gauze pads is applied to the abdominal wound.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 90.
    claim 2 is for the extra gauze pads.
    the HCPCS code of the extra gauze pads is "A6216".
    the extra gauze pads is applied to the abdominal wound.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 100.
    the abdominal wound is a wound of Gus.
    the type of the abdominal wound is surgical wound.
    claim 3 is for the absorptive dressings.
    the HCPCS code of the absorptive dressings is "A6254".
    the absorptive dressings is applied to the left hip ulcer.
    the absorptive dressings is applied to the right hip ulcer.
    claim 3 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 3 is 30.
    claim 4 is for the extra absorptive dressings.
    the HCPCS code of the extra absorptive dressings is "A6254".
    the extra absorptive dressings is applied to the left hip ulcer.
    the extra absorptive dressings is applied to the right hip ulcer.
    claim 4 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 4 is 40.
    the left hip ulcer is a wound of Hal.
    the type of the left hip ulcer is pressure ulcer.
    the stage of the left hip ulcer is 3.
    the exudate of the left hip ulcer is heavy.
    the right hip ulcer is a wound of Hal.
    the type of the right hip ulcer is pressure ulcer.
    the stage of the right hip ulcer is 3.
    the exudate of the right hip ulcer is moderate.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 3 is reasonable and necessary"].

% Collagen on a partial thickness venous ulcer with minimal exudate: covered
% only if the wound has stalled — so the treating practitioner finds for
% Ida (claim 1, covered); the Council finds Jo's record shows steady
% healing (claim 2, not covered — the Council-style unsupported finding).
scenario collagen_stalled is:
    claim 1 is for the collagen dressings.
    the HCPCS code of the collagen dressings is "A6021".
    the collagen dressings is applied to the ankle ulcer.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 8.
    the ankle ulcer is a wound of Ida.
    the type of the ankle ulcer is venous insufficiency ulcer.
    the thickness of the ankle ulcer is partial thickness.
    the exudate of the ankle ulcer is minimal.
    the finding wound healing stalled about Ida is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "the ulcer area has not decreased in six weeks of compression and moist dressings".
    claim 2 is for the second collagen dressings.
    the HCPCS code of the second collagen dressings is "A6021".
    the second collagen dressings is applied to the shin ulcer.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 8.
    the shin ulcer is a wound of Jo.
    the type of the shin ulcer is venous insufficiency ulcer.
    the thickness of the shin ulcer is partial thickness.
    the exudate of the shin ulcer is minimal.
    the finding wound healing stalled about Jo is unsupported, according to the Council, because "the weekly measurements in the nursing notes show the ulcer shrinking from 12 to 7 square centimeters during the month before the collagen was ordered".
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].

% Combinations: a hydrogel filler and an alginate filler on Kim's cavity
% (claims 1 and 2, neither covered); tape over Lee's bordered foam cover,
% the reviewer finding no reason for it (claim 3, not covered); tape holding
% Mia's gauze pads, which have no border (claim 4, covered).
scenario combinations is:
    claim 1 is for the hydrogel filler.
    the HCPCS code of the hydrogel filler is "A6248".
    the hydrogel filler is applied to the cavity.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 3.
    claim 2 is for the alginate filler.
    the HCPCS code of the alginate filler is "A6199".
    the alginate filler is applied to the cavity.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 30.
    the cavity is a wound of Kim.
    the type of the cavity is pressure ulcer.
    the stage of the cavity is 4.
    the exudate of the cavity is moderate.
    claim 3 is for the tape.
    the HCPCS code of the tape is "A4450".
    the tape is applied to the back ulcer.
    the tape is an accessory of the bordered foam.
    the tape is furnished to Lee.
    claim 3 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 3 is 24.
    the HCPCS code of the bordered foam is "A6212".
    the bordered foam is applied to the back ulcer.
    the bordered foam is furnished to Lee.
    the back ulcer is a wound of Lee.
    the finding additional tape necessary about Lee is unsupported, according to the reviewer, because "the record gives no reason for tape over a cover with an adhesive border".
    claim 4 is for the second tape.
    the HCPCS code of the second tape is "A4450".
    the second tape is applied to the arm wound.
    the second tape is an accessory of the plain gauze.
    the second tape is furnished to Mia.
    claim 4 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 4 is 24.
    the HCPCS code of the plain gauze is "A6216".
    the plain gauze is applied to the arm wound.
    the plain gauze is furnished to Mia.
    the arm wound is a wound of Mia.
    the type of the arm wound is surgical wound.
    rn expects answers ["claim 4 is reasonable and necessary"].

% Bandages and wraps for Ned's venous stasis ulcer: a zinc paste bandage
% weekly (claim 1, covered); a high compression bandage of a multi-layer
% system, changed twice a week (claim 2, covered: no weekly limit in a
% multi-layer system); a light compression bandage used as the primary
% dressing (claim 3, not covered); a gradient compression wrap for six
% months (claim 4, covered), two for the one leg (claim 5, not covered).
scenario compression is:
    claim 1 is for the zinc bandages.
    the HCPCS code of the zinc bandages is "A6456".
    the zinc bandages is applied to the stasis ulcer.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 4.
    claim 2 is for the compression bandages.
    the HCPCS code of the compression bandages is "A6451".
    the compression bandages is applied to the stasis ulcer.
    the compression bandages is part of a multi-layer compression bandage system.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 8.
    claim 3 is for the light bandages.
    the HCPCS code of the light bandages is "A6448".
    the light bandages is applied to the stasis ulcer.
    the use of the light bandages is primary dressing.
    claim 3 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 3 is 4.
    claim 4 is for the wrap.
    the HCPCS code of the wrap is "A6545".
    the wrap is applied to the stasis ulcer.
    claim 4 is for a supply of 6 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 4 is 1.
    claim 5 is for the extra wraps.
    the HCPCS code of the extra wraps is "A6545".
    the extra wraps is applied to the stasis ulcer.
    claim 5 is for a supply of 6 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 5 is 2.
    the stasis ulcer is a wound of Ned.
    the type of the stasis ulcer is venous insufficiency ulcer.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 2 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 4 is reasonable and necessary"].

% The complete claim whose last wound evaluation is 50 days old, with no
% reason given (Policy Article A54563): reasonable and necessary, not
% payable; the flip adds the record's justification.
scenario stale_evaluation is:
    claim 1 is for the foam covers.
    the HCPCS code of the foam covers is "A6212".
    the foam covers is furnished to Ann.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 12.
    the use of the foam covers is primary dressing.
    the foam covers is applied to the sacral ulcer.
    the sacral ulcer is a wound of Ann.
    the type of the sacral ulcer is pressure ulcer.
    the stage of the sacral ulcer is 3.
    the exudate of the sacral ulcer is moderate.
    the sacral ulcer has been debrided.
    the sacral ulcer was evaluated on 2026-01-10.
    the order for the foam covers is dated 2026-02-20.
    the order for the foam covers is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the foam covers includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the foam covers includes order date.
    the order for the foam covers includes description of the item.
    the order for the foam covers includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the foam covers includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the foam covers was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-22.
    the foam covers was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the foam covers.
    pay expects answers [].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at remedy"].
    flip expects changes [["add: the medical record justifies the late evaluation of the sacral ulcer with the other monitoring methods used"]].

% A skin tear neither surgically treated nor debrided: gauze pads are
